Q: Building a custom mask in Next.js A: <p>The easiest way is <strong>not to mask the purple section itself</strong>. Instead, create the concave corners using pseudo-elements that match the parent background, then place the circle above everything.</p>
<p>For your layout, I’d use this approach:</p>
<pre><code>import { ChevronDown } from &quot;lucide-react&quot;;

export function PostQueueBoard() {
  return (
    &lt;div className=&quot;relative w-full max-w-md rounded-xl border border-gray-800 bg-gray-900 p-6&quot;&gt;

      {/* Top section */}
      &lt;div
        className=&quot;
          relative flex h-[84px] w-full items-center justify-between
          rounded-xl p-4
          after:absolute after:-bottom-[14px] after:left-1/2
          after:h-[28px] after:w-[28px] after:-translate-x-1/2
          after:rounded-full after:bg-gray-900
        &quot;
        style={{
          background: &quot;linear-gradient(90deg, #D6B7F1, #7B2FBE)&quot;,
        }}
      &gt;
        &lt;div className=&quot;flex flex-col text-white&quot;&gt;
          &lt;span className=&quot;font-bold&quot;&gt;Total In Queue&lt;/span&gt;
          &lt;span className=&quot;text-sm&quot;&gt;1 currently processing&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;

        &lt;span className=&quot;text-4xl font-bold text-white&quot;&gt;24&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;

      {/* Concave cutout */}
      &lt;div
        className=&quot;
          pointer-events-none absolute left-1/2 top-[108px]
          z-10 h-[32px] w-[64px] -translate-x-1/2
          bg-gray-900
          [border-radius:0_0_50%_50%]
        &quot;
      /&gt;

      {/* Circle */}
      &lt;div className=&quot;absolute left-1/2 top-[108px] z-20 -translate-x-1/2 -translate-y-1/2&quot;&gt;
        &lt;div
          className=&quot;flex h-[28px] w-[28px] items-center justify-center rounded-full shadow-lg&quot;
          style={{
            background: &quot;linear-gradient(90deg, #B183D9, #9E64D1)&quot;,
          }}
        &gt;
          &lt;ChevronDown className=&quot;text-white&quot; size={16} /&gt;
        &lt;/div&gt;
      &lt;/div&gt;

      {/* Bottom section */}
      &lt;div className=&quot;mt-2 min-h-[120px] rounded-xl bg-white p-4&quot;&gt;
        {/* Section Content */}
      &lt;/div&gt;
    &lt;/div&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>

Q: Why is the terminal outputting &quot;0&quot; in this code? A: <pre><code>The reason your code is outputting `0` (or running into logic issues) comes down to how the variables and the loop are set up:

1. **`fibonnaci` is never updated inside the loop:** Your `fibonnaci` variable is calculated just once before the `for` loop begins. Inside the loop, you only increment `n`, so the condition `fibonnaci % 2 == 0` evaluates against the exact same number over and over, rather than generating a new sequence.
2. **The loop logic:** A `for` loop running up to `4,000,000` counter steps will not track the value of the Fibonacci terms. Instead, you want your loop to continue *while* the Fibonacci value itself is less than or equal to `4,000,000` (`while (fib &lt;= 4000000)`).

### How to fix it:
For Project Euler Problem 2, it is much easier to use a `while` loop that generates the actual sequence step-by-step until it hits the 4 million limit, keeping track of the previous two terms. Here is a working C++ approach:

```cpp

    #include &lt;iostream&gt;

    using namespace std;

    int main() {
        long long sum = 0;
        long long prev = 1;      // First term
        long long current = 2;   // Second term

        while (current &lt;= 4000000) {
            if (current % 2 == 0) {
                sum += current;
            }

            // Generate the next Fibonacci term
            long long next = prev + current;
            prev = current;
            current = next;
        }

        cout &lt;&lt; &quot;Sum of even Fibonacci terms: &quot; &lt;&lt; sum &lt;&lt; endl;
        return 0;
    }
</code></pre>
